  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "Let $L$ be the length and d be the diameter of cross-section of a wire. Wires of the same material with different $L$ and $d$ are subjected to the same tension along the length of the wire. In which of the following cases, the extension of wire will be the maximum?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "kueuj",
      "text": "$L=200 \\mathrm{cm}, \\mathrm{d}=0.5 \\mathrm{mm}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "BKaZU",
      "text": "$L=300 \\mathrm{cm}, \\mathrm{d}=1.0 \\mathrm{mm}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "wBOBB",
      "text": "$L=50 \\mathrm{cm}, \\quad d=0.05 \\mathrm{mm}$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "yxB6Y",
      "text": "$L=100 \\mathrm{cm}, \\mathrm{d}=0.2 \\mathrm{mm}$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "We know\r\n$$\r\n\\Delta l=\\frac{F \\times l}{A \\times Y}=\\frac{4 L}{\\pi d^{2}}\r\n$$\r\nHere $\\frac{4}{\\pi}$ is a constant.\r\nHence, $\\Delta l \\propto \\frac{L}{d^{2}}$",
